<header class="hero grid-bg">
  <div class="hero-inner">
    <div class="hero-tag mono">A GUIDE FOR STARTING AT ZERO</div>
    <h1>Math isn't one<br>subject. It's <em>six</em><br>foundations.</h1>
    <p class="lede">You don't need to already be "good at math" to use this. You need a map of what math actually is, so that every class, textbook chapter, and real-world problem has a place to land. This is that map.</p>
    <div class="hero-meta">
      <div>
        <div class="num mono">01</div>
        <p>Six pillars, explained in plain language — no jargon assumed.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="num mono">02</div>
        <p>Why each one shows up in college courses and everyday decisions.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="num mono">03</div>
        <p>A dependency map showing what to learn first, and why.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<section id="why">
  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>Why think in pillars, not chapters</h2>
    <div class="index mono">SECTION — 01</div>
  </div>
  <div class="intro-columns">
    <div>
      <p>Most people's experience of math is a pile of disconnected topics: fractions one year, quadratic equations the next, then suddenly "statistics" shows up with no warning. It feels like a random obstacle course.</p>
      <p>It isn't. Underneath the courses, math is built from a small number of <strong>ways of thinking</strong> — pillars that hold up everything else. Arithmetic holds up algebra. Algebra holds up almost every course that comes after it. Statistics holds up how you'll read the news, a medical study, or a spreadsheet at work.</p>
      <p>Once you can name the pillar a problem belongs to, the problem stops being a mystery. You know what tools it's asking you to use, and you know which foundation to go strengthen if it isn't clicking.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="callout">"You are never bad at math. You are, at most, missing one pillar underneath the one you're standing on."</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="pillars">
  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>The six pillars</h2>
    <div class="index mono">SECTION — 02</div>
  </div>
  <p class="section-intro">Every pillar below includes what it actually is, why it matters in college and outside it, the core skills inside it, and one small example so the idea isn't abstract.</p>

  <!-- PILLAR 1 -->
  <div class="pillar">
    <div class="pillar-visual">
      <span class="tag">PILLAR 01 / FOUNDATION</span>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 200 100" fill="none">
        <circle cx="30" cy="70" r="10" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <circle cx="60" cy="70" r="10" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <circle cx="90" cy="70" r="10" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <line x1="115" y1="30" x2="115" y2="90" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <path d="M135 40 h40 M135 60 h40 M135 80 h30" stroke-width="1.4"/>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <div class="pillar-body">
      <h3><span class="no mono">01</span>Arithmetic &amp; Number Sense</h3>
      <p class="pillar-def">The math of quantities: adding, subtracting, multiplying, dividing, and understanding what fractions, decimals, and percentages actually represent. It's not "easy math for kids" — it's the operating system every other pillar runs on.</p>
      <div class="pillar-grid">
        <div>
          <h4>Why it matters</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Every college formula eventually reduces to arithmetic</li>
            <li>Budgeting, tipping, taxes, interest, unit prices</li>
            <li>Reading measurements, dosages, recipes, discounts</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4>Core skills</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>The four operations, done confidently without a calculator</li>
            <li>Fractions, decimals, percentages, and converting between them</li>
            <li>Estimating an answer before calculating it exactly</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="example-box">
        <span class="label mono">TRY THIS</span>
        <p>A jacket is $80, marked 25% off, plus 7% sales tax. Before you calculate, estimate: 25% off means you're paying about three-quarters, so around $60, plus a little tax — call it $64. (Exact answer: $64.20.) That instinct to estimate first is number sense.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- PILLAR 2 -->
  <div class="pillar">
    <div class="pillar-visual">
      <span class="tag">PILLAR 02 / STRUCTURE</span>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 200 100" fill="none">
        <rect x="20" y="35" width="30" height="30" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <text x="30" y="55" font-family="IBM Plex Mono" font-size="14" fill="#e3a441" stroke="none">x</text>
        <path d="M58 50 h20" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <path d="M78 44 l6 6 l-6 6" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <rect x="95" y="35" width="30" height="30" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <text x="103" y="55" font-family="IBM Plex Mono" font-size="14" fill="#e3a441" stroke="none">7</text>
        <path d="M133 50 h20" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <path d="M153 44 l6 6 l-6 6" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <rect x="170" y="35" width="20" height="30" stroke-width="1.4"/>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <div class="pillar-body">
      <h3><span class="no mono">02</span>Algebra</h3>
      <p class="pillar-def">The language of unknowns and relationships. Instead of working with fixed numbers, algebra lets you work with quantities that change or aren't known yet — and describes how they relate to each other with equations.</p>
      <div class="pillar-grid">
        <div>
          <h4>Why it matters</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>The entry ticket to nearly every college math, science, and business course</li>
            <li>Spreadsheets, budgeting formulas, and "what if" planning</li>
            <li>Any situation where one thing depends on another (pay per hour, distance per gallon)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4>Core skills</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Solving for an unknown value in an equation</li>
            <li>Reading and writing formulas (y depends on x)</li>
            <li>Working with variables, exponents, and simple functions</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="example-box">
        <span class="label mono">TRY THIS</span>
        <p>You earn $15 an hour and need $300 for a flight. The unknown is hours worked, call it h. The relationship is 15h = 300. Algebra tells you how to isolate h: divide both sides by 15, so h = 20 hours.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- PILLAR 3 -->
  <div class="pillar">
    <div class="pillar-visual">
      <span class="tag">PILLAR 03 / SPACE</span>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 200 100" fill="none">
        <polygon points="100,20 170,80 30,80" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <line x1="100" y1="20" x2="100" y2="80" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>
        <path d="M95 78 h10 v-8" stroke-width="1"/>
        <circle cx="100" cy="20" r="2" fill="#e3a441" stroke="none"/>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <div class="pillar-body">
      <h3><span class="no mono">03</span>Geometry &amp; Spatial Reasoning</h3>
      <p class="pillar-def">The math of shape, space, size, and position — how far, how big, what angle, what fits where. It trains you to reason visually and precisely about the physical world.</p>
      <div class="pillar-grid">
        <div>
          <h4>Why it matters</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Architecture, engineering, design, and construction all run on it</li>
            <li>Reading maps, floor plans, and furniture measurements</li>
            <li>The visual reasoning behind computer graphics and navigation apps</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4>Core skills</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Area, perimeter, volume, and angles</li>
            <li>The Pythagorean theorem and basic trigonometry</li>
            <li>Visualizing 2D and 3D shapes and how they transform</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="example-box">
        <span class="label mono">TRY THIS</span>
        <p>You want a rug to fit a 10 ft by 12 ft room with a 1 ft border of bare floor on every side. The rug needs to be 8 ft by 10 ft — geometry is subtracting the border from both dimensions, not just guessing a size that "looks right."</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- PILLAR 4 -->
  <div class="pillar">
    <div class="pillar-visual">
      <span class="tag">PILLAR 04 / DATA</span>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 200 100" fill="none">
        <line x1="25" y1="85" x2="25" y2="20" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <line x1="25" y1="85" x2="180" y2="85" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <rect x="45" y="55" width="18" height="30" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <rect x="80" y="35" width="18" height="50" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <rect x="115" y="65" width="18" height="20" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <rect x="150" y="45" width="18" height="40" stroke-width="1.4"/>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <div class="pillar-body">
      <h3><span class="no mono">04</span>Statistics &amp; Probability</h3>
      <p class="pillar-def">The math of data and uncertainty: summarizing information, spotting patterns, and reasoning about chance when outcomes aren't guaranteed. It's how you separate a real trend from noise.</p>
      <div class="pillar-grid">
        <div>
          <h4>Why it matters</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Required in psychology, business, health sciences, and most social sciences</li>
            <li>Reading polls, studies, news headlines, and product reviews critically</li>
            <li>Evaluating risk: insurance, investing, medical decisions, weather</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4>Core skills</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Mean, median, and how outliers distort an average</li>
            <li>Reading charts and distributions accurately</li>
            <li>Basic probability: chance, odds, and independence</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="example-box">
        <span class="label mono">TRY THIS</span>
        <p>A headline says "average salary at this company is $120,000." If the CEO earns $2 million and nine employees earn $40,000, the average is pulled way up. The median (the middle value) would tell a very different, more honest story.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- PILLAR 5 -->
  <div class="pillar">
    <div class="pillar-visual">
      <span class="tag">PILLAR 05 / METHOD</span>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 200 100" fill="none">
        <circle cx="35" cy="50" r="14" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <path d="M49 50 h30" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <rect x="79" y="36" width="28" height="28" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <path d="M107 50 h30" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <polygon points="152,32 172,50 152,68 132,50" stroke-width="1.4"/>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <div class="pillar-body">
      <h3><span class="no mono">05</span>Logical &amp; Analytical Reasoning</h3>
      <p class="pillar-def">Not a topic so much as a habit of mind: breaking a problem into smaller pieces, following an argument step by step, and checking whether a conclusion actually follows from its premises. This is the pillar that makes you good at every other one.</p>
      <div class="pillar-grid">
        <div>
          <h4>Why it matters</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Underlies proof-writing, computer science, and philosophy courses</li>
            <li>Spotting flawed arguments in ads, debates, and contracts</li>
            <li>Debugging: in code, in a recipe that failed, in a broken plan</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4>Core skills</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>If-then reasoning and identifying assumptions</li>
            <li>Breaking a large problem into smaller, ordered steps</li>
            <li>Checking an answer for reasonableness, not just correctness</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="example-box">
        <span class="label mono">TRY THIS</span>
        <p>"Every dog I've met is friendly, so all dogs are friendly." Logical reasoning asks: does the evidence actually support the conclusion, or just a small piece of it? (It doesn't — this is a classic overgeneralization.)</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- PILLAR 6 -->
  <div class="pillar">
    <div class="pillar-visual">
      <span class="tag">PILLAR 06 / CHANGE</span>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 200 100" fill="none">
        <line x1="20" y1="85" x2="20" y2="15" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <line x1="20" y1="85" x2="185" y2="85" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <path d="M25 80 C 70 80, 90 20, 160 20" stroke-width="1.4" fill="none"/>
        <line x1="90" y1="55" x2="130" y2="41" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="2 3"/>
        <circle cx="90" cy="55" r="2.5" fill="#e3a441" stroke="none"/>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <div class="pillar-body">
      <h3><span class="no mono">06</span>Calculus &amp; Rates of Change</h3>
      <p class="pillar-def">The math of things that are changing: how fast, how much, and where change speeds up or slows down. It sits on top of algebra and geometry, so it's usually the last pillar people meet — but the idea behind it is simpler than the name suggests.</p>
      <div class="pillar-grid">
        <div>
          <h4>Why it matters</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Required for engineering, physics, economics, and many business majors</li>
            <li>Explains acceleration, growth rates, and "the rate is slowing down" news stories</li>
            <li>The math underneath optimization: the best price, the fastest route, the least waste</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4>Core skills</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Understanding rate of change (how fast something is moving or growing)</li>
            <li>Reading a graph's shape: rising, falling, speeding up, leveling off</li>
            <li>The basic idea of a limit — what a value approaches, even if it never quite arrives</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="example-box">
        <span class="label mono">TRY THIS</span>
        <p>A car's speedometer isn't measuring total distance — it's measuring how fast distance is changing right now. That instant rate of change is the entire idea behind calculus, before any formulas get involved.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

