<section id="practice">
  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>How to actually learn this, from zero</h2>
    <div class="index mono">SECTION — 04</div>
  </div>
  <p class="section-intro">Knowing the map matters less than knowing how to walk it. These are the habits that move someone from "I'm not a math person" to genuinely comfortable — regardless of where they're starting from.</p>

  <div class="practice-list">
    <div class="practice-item">
      <h4>Start below your level, not at it</h4>
      <p>If algebra feels shaky, the fix is almost never "study algebra harder." It's usually a gap in arithmetic or fractions. Go one pillar down, get that solid, and the level above gets noticeably easier.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="practice-item">
      <h4>Do problems by hand before using a calculator</h4>
      <p>A calculator gives you an answer; working by hand builds the number sense that lets you catch a wrong answer later. Save the calculator for checking, not thinking.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="practice-item">
      <h4>Practice a little, often, not a lot, rarely</h4>
      <p>Twenty minutes a day beats one three-hour session before a test. Math skills fade fast without light, repeated use — spacing it out is what makes it stick.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="practice-item">
      <h4>Get the wrong answer, then find out why</h4>
      <p>Mistakes are the fastest source of information you have. Redoing a wrong problem and finding the exact step that broke teaches more than ten correct ones done on autopilot.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="practice-item">
      <h4>Attach every abstract idea to something real</h4>
      <p>A variable is easier to hold onto as "hours worked" than as "x." Before memorizing a formula, find one situation in your own life where it applies.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="practice-item">
      <h4>Explain it to someone who knows less than you</h4>
      <p>If you can explain a concept in plain words to a beginner, you actually understand it. If you can only repeat the formula, you don't yet — and that's fine, it just tells you what to revisit.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<footer>
  <p class="fnote">There is no such thing as a "math brain" you're either born with or without. Every pillar here is a learnable skill, built the same way any skill is: in small, repeated, honest attempts.</p>
  <p class="fmark mono">THE FOUNDATIONS — A STARTING MAP, NOT THE WHOLE TERRITORY</p>
